INT. WAX MUSEUM - BACK STOREROOM - NIGHT

Shelves of disembodied heads. A half-melted Napoleon lies on a cart. A bucket labelled "SPARE ELVIS."

GREGORY PUFFIN, 50s, the curator, in a velvet jacket two sizes too small, wrings his hands. MAXINE TAN, 30s, a night guard with a flashlight and a dead-eyed stare. ULYSSES CRANE, 40s, a restorer in a paint-stained smock, kneels by a headless Churchill. BABS DELANEY, 60s, the cleaning lady, mops in slow circles. KOFI BOATENG, 20s, a tour guide in a tricorn hat, holds a tray of sandwiches.

GREGORY
This is a disaster.

MAXINE
It's a Tuesday.

GREGORY
The Queen is missing.

ULYSSES
The Queen is always missing. We keep losing her wig.

GREGORY
Not the wig. The QUEEN.

Everyone turns. A single empty pedestal stands in the corner, a velvet rope still looped around it.

BABS
I thought she was in the shop.

KOFI
I thought she was in the Hall of Kings.

MAXINE
Nobody checked the Hall of Kings.

GREGORY
Nobody check ANYTHING. Everyone stay calm.

A loud clunk from the ceiling. A pale shape swings past on a rope. They scatter, Babs shrieking, Kofi dropping the tray, Ulysses diving behind the cart, Maxine drawing her flashlight like a pistol.

The shape stops dead in the middle of the room, swaying: a wax Queen Elizabeth, hung by her own sash from a rafter.

Silence. Kofi peers from behind the cart.

KOFI
Is she... meant to be there?

BABS
(sniffing)
It's the best she's looked in years.

GREGORY
(faintly)
Who did this?

A muffled giggle from a tall wardrobe. The door creaks open. A tiny figure steps out in a toddler's tricorn hat, holding an enormous pair of scissors.

Everyone freezes.

GREGORY (CONT'D)
Kofi. Is that your niece?

KOFI
(grimly)
Take-Your-Child-To-Work Day.
